WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.463
Typing long column names can be tiring.

2
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:06.507
In this lesson, you will learn a shortcut:

3
00:00:06.507 --> 00:00:09.932
selecting columns by their position number, with square brackets.

4
00:00:09.932 --> 00:00:16.020
You will also learn the colon, which picks a whole range at once. Let's try it.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.376
Here is the dataset in View.

6
00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:26.152
Column one is Single Response, column two is Province Name, three is Sex of

7
00:00:26.152 --> 00:00:29.040
household head, and four is Marital Status.

8
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.583
Square brackets work like an address:

9
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:42.858
first the rows, then a comma, then the columns.

10
00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:46.860
If we leave the rows empty, R takes all the rows.

11
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:52.892
So:

12
00:00:52.892 --> 00:01:00.280
Demographic underscore one, arrow, Dataset, open square bracket, comma, c, two, three, four, close bracket.

13
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.297
Run it, and the new table has three hundred fifty two rows, and three columns:

14
00:01:18.297 --> 00:01:20.262
province, sex, and marital status.

15
00:01:20.262 --> 00:01:23.800
The same result as select, with much less typing.

16
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.631
Now a second table, called Main.

17
00:01:34.631 --> 00:01:38.720
Inside c, I write two, three, and then six, colon, ten.

18
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.474
The colon means:

19
00:01:53.474 --> 00:01:54.907
from six to ten.

20
00:01:54.907 --> 00:01:59.920
So we get columns six, seven, eight, nine, and ten, without typing each one.

21
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.178
Run it.

22
00:02:08.178 --> 00:02:09.735
Main has seven columns.

23
00:02:09.735 --> 00:02:12.069
Open it, and there they are:

24
00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:15.960
province, sex, the land questions, and the wage labor answers.

25
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.686
A small warning:

26
00:02:48.686 --> 00:02:52.117
positions change if columns are added or removed.

27
00:02:52.117 --> 00:02:55.547
Names are safer for scripts you will reuse.

28
00:02:55.547 --> 00:02:58.120
Positions are great for quick looks.

29
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.931
Next, a new note:

30
00:03:08.931 --> 00:03:13.143
to bring changes in a single or more values or cells.

31
00:03:13.143 --> 00:03:18.503
Before we change anything, I use table to count the values in the Sex

32
00:03:18.503 --> 00:03:20.800
of household head column. Nice work!

